<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Parle needs the microphone to transcribe your dictation, entirely on this device.</string>

    <!--
      LAN sync. Both keys are required and neither was here.

      macOS 14 and later gate local-network access. Without
      NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription the prompt has no explanation to show, and
      without NSBonjourServices the system filters the app's mDNS traffic
      outright: browsing returns nothing and registration is not seen by
      anyone. The failure is SILENT, discovery reports no error, it simply
      finds no peers for ever, so sync would have appeared to work and then
      never found the other machine, which is the first thing anyone would try.

      NSBonjourServices must list the service type WITHOUT the trailing
      ".local.", so it reads "_parle._tcp" against
      parle_sync::discovery::SERVICE_TYPE ("_parle._tcp.local.").

      The service type still says parle rather than parle. It is the wire
      identifier two installs must agree on, so renaming it is a protocol
      break for no user-visible gain; it is deliberately left alone.
    -->
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Parle finds your other devices on this network so your dictation and clipboard history can sync directly between them. Nothing is sent to the internet.</string>
    <key>NSBonjourServices</key>
    <array>
        <string>_parle._tcp</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
